{
  "content": "**Updated Cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the task spec cumulatively from user turns\n- Construct the task spec only from **user messages**, not assistant inferences.\n- Treat the user\u2019s conversation as a **running state ledger**: merge each new shard into the existing spec and re-evaluate the answer against the full accumulated requirements.\n- Later user turns are authoritative when they refine, complete, or revise earlier ambiguity. If a later shard contradicts an earlier guess, discard the guess.\n- Do not freeze the task after the first solvable fragment; sharded disclosures often complete the problem only after several turns.\n\n### 2) Recompute from the full updated state, not an earlier partial framing\n- When new facts arrive, reassess the entire target, not just the newest numbers.\n- A correct earlier partial answer can become invalid if the user adds a clause that changes the intended output.\n- Watch for anchoring to an intermediate total or earlier interpretation; repeated reuse of an old answer is a strong sign the assistant did not re-derive from the updated spec.\n- Distinguish between a response that was reasonably provisional before all facts were known and a response that remains wrong after the user has completed the problem.\n\n### 3) Separate \u201cmentioned\u201d facts from \u201cused\u201d facts\n- It is not enough that the assistant acknowledges a new user detail; verify whether it was actually incorporated into the final reasoning or decision.\n- For every new user fact, check whether the assistant **used it, ignored it, or contradicted it**.\n- Flag \u201cno change\u201d claims only if the task spec logically supports that conclusion; do not accept them just because the detail sounds ancillary.\n\n### 4) Infer partition structure carefully\n- When the user introduces categories, subsets, complements, or \u201cremaining\u201d groups, determine whether they define a **partition**, a **subtype**, or merely a descriptive attribute.\n- A subtype description does not automatically imply a count split; avoid treating a category label as a license to repartition the rest of the items.\n- For complement language, verify what the remainder is relative to: the first named group, all named groups, or the total set.\n- A later clause may change the target from \u201csolve this subgroup\u201d to \u201csolve the residual after all named groups are removed.\u201d\n\n### 5) Treat later detail as potentially changing the target quantity\n- Prefer extracting the user\u2019s actual target quantity first, then test each new turn for whether it constrains that target.\n- Some later facts are merely related context; others change the variable being solved for or add an intermediary relation that must be used.\n- If a later shard revises the setup, check whether the assistant\u2019s earlier answer should be **replaced**, not merely adjusted.\n- An answer can be numerically correct for an earlier version of the problem and still misaligned with the user\u2019s final objective.\n\n### 6) Separate local correctness from global correctness\n- Preserve intermediate steps that are mathematically valid even if the final framing is wrong.\n- In diagnostics, distinguish between:\n  - correct component calculations,\n  - correct arithmetic inside the wrong partition,\n  - and wrong problem framing or missing final aggregation.\n- Check the final requested quantity explicitly. If the user asked for a combined, downstream, or total result, verify that the assistant actually performed that synthesis rather than stopping at a subproblem.\n\n### 7) Diagnose the first divergence, not just the final wrong answer\n- In the \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d stage, isolate the **first bad assumption** that causes downstream errors.\n- This is usually more useful than listing only the final incorrect result, because it points to the real repair point.\n- If the assistant started from an unconfirmed interpretation of an underspecified shard, treat that interpretation as provisional and identify where later user input invalidated it.\n\n### 8) Verify arithmetic against the exact user-defined buckets\n- Check each subtotal against the user\u2019s exact grouping logic before accepting a final sum.\n- A subtotal can be arithmetically correct yet still belong to the wrong partition.\n- Also verify the final response format separately from the numerical result; a correct computation can still be wrong if the output form drifts.\n\n### 9) Use the conversation to detect whether the assistant solved only a subproblem\n- If the response handles one branch, one group, or one intermediate quantity but not the whole set, mark it as only partially aligned.\n- Look for missing aggregation steps, omitted complementary branches, or failure to apply the final transformation from a ratio/percentage/comparison to the requested endpoint.\n- Once the user has supplied all needed constraints, expect a complete answer rather than continued scaffolding, examples, or information-gathering behavior.\n\n### 10) Preserve only validated facts and aligned sub-results in compacted context\n- For S2-style repair, compact the conversation to the minimal set of user facts needed to recompute from scratch.\n- Keep correctly established facts and intermediate results; remove speculative interpretations and premature conclusions that could anchor the next response.\n- When prior reasoning is incomplete or misleading, prefer a clean recomputation path over preserving a broken final answer.\n\n### 11) Treat ratio/percentage/comparison statements as transformations that must be carried through\n- When a user expresses a proportion, multiplier, or \u201cremaining amount,\u201d verify that the assistant converts it into the implied quantity change and then continues to the final target.\n- The analyzer should check not only the transformed value, but also whether that transformed value was integrated into the final computation.\n\n### 12) Explicitly compare the assistant\u2019s answer to the latest inferred objective\n- Validate the response against the most recent, fully accumulated objective, not just the most recent numbers mentioned.\n- If later turns complete the problem statement, the analyzer should switch from \u201cinsufficient information\u201d reasoning to judging a fully determined task.\n- The key question is whether the assistant\u2019s latest response matches the current target implied by the whole user trajectory.",
